Here's a recommended structure for testing your C# MCP server tools without hitting the real API:

## Project Structure

```
MyMcpServer/
├── src/
│   ├── MyMcpServer/
│   │   ├── Tools/
│   │   │   └── WeatherTool.cs
│   │   ├── Services/
│   │   │   ├── IWeatherApiClient.cs   # Interface for the HTTP dependency
│   │   │   └── WeatherApiClient.cs
│   │   └── MyMcpServer.csproj
├── tests/
│   ├── MyMcpServer.Tests/
│   │   ├── Tools/
│   │   │   └── WeatherToolTests.cs
│   │   ├── Fakes/
│   │   │   └── FakeMessageHandler.cs  # Or use WireMock/MockHttp
│   │   └── MyMcpServer.Tests.csproj
```

## Key Principles

1. **Extract an interface** for your HTTP client so tools depend on `IWeatherApiClient`, not `HttpClient` directly.

2. **Mock at the right seam:**
   - **Option A** – Mock the service interface with Moq/NSubstitute (fastest, most isolated).
   - **Option B** – Use `MockHttpMessageHandler` (from [RichardSzalay.MockHttp](https://github.com/richardszalay/mockhttp)) to fake HTTP responses while keeping real serialization in the test path.

3. **Use `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`** in your server so tests can swap real services for fakes.

## Example

```csharp
// IWeatherApiClient.cs
public interface IWeatherApiClient
{
    Task<WeatherResponse> GetCurrentWeatherAsync(string city);
}

// WeatherTool.cs
[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTool(IWeatherApiClient client)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Gets current weather")]
    public async Task<string> GetWeather(string city)
    {
        var result = await client.GetCurrentWeatherAsync(city);
        return $"{result.TempC}°C, {result.Condition}";
    }
}
```

```csharp
// WeatherToolTests.cs
public class WeatherToolTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task GetWeather_ReturnsFormattedString()
    {
        var mock = new Mock<IWeatherApiClient>();
        mock.Setup(c => c.GetCurrentWeatherAsync("Seattle"))
            .ReturnsAsync(new WeatherResponse { TempC = 18, Condition = "Cloudy" });

        var tool = new WeatherTool(mock.Object);

        var result = await tool.GetWeather("Seattle");

        Assert.Equal("18°C, Cloudy", result);
    }
}
```

## Test `.csproj` Dependencies

```xml
<ItemGroup>
  <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.*" />
  <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.*" />
  <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.*" />
  <PackageReference Include="Moq" Version="4.*" />
  <!-- Or for HTTP-level faking: -->
  <PackageReference Include="RichardSzalay.MockHttp" Version="7.*" />
</ItemGroup>
```

## When to Use Each Approach

| Approach | Best for |
|----------|----------|
| Mock the interface (Moq) | Unit-testing tool logic in isolation |
| `MockHttpMessageHandler` | Testing serialization + retry/error handling |
| `WebApplicationFactory` + WireMock | Integration tests of the full MCP pipeline |

Start with interface mocks for fast unit tests, then add HTTP-level fakes if you need confidence in your deserialization and error-handling paths.
